Agents MUST preserve private records and MUST NOT publish secrets. Network access and any external write require explicit owner approval.

## Compatibility
Changes MUST explain format compatibility, migration and recovery. Existing readable exports remain supported unless an approved direction explicitly changes that guarantee.

## Verification
Run the project-owned applicable commands before completion claims; report actual results and limitations. Local success MUST NOT be called hosted CI success.

## Review
A contributor MUST NOT independently approve their own contribution. Required independent review precedes reliance; final verification remains distinct.

## Documentation
Affected living Designs and user-facing guidance MUST agree with changed behavior. Link detailed procedures at their owners rather than duplicating them here.

## Agent behavior
Preserve unrelated work, disclose missing evidence and material assumptions, and avoid unrelated refactoring. A map reports observed state, not future architecture or execution commitments.

## Standard workflow and manual use
Follow docs/workflow.md for the selected standard lifecycle. Isolated manual skills remain bounded; writing an artifact alone grants no approval, continuation or external permission.
","62c4a87619d2cdd9379f41602b27e5eb1f18be15be9655155c50aad134c88cdd":"# Ledger Garden

## Pitch

Ledger Garden is a local-first record organizer for small volunteer groups. It keeps their lists useful without an account by importing, checking and storing records on their own machine.

## What makes this different

Unlike hosted spreadsheets, Ledger Garden works without a network account. Groups retain direct control of their records, accepting fewer collaboration features in return.


## Who it is for

Volunteer coordinators who maintain modest lists on one shared computer and need readable, exportable records.

## Who it is not for

Large organizations needing simultaneous remote editing, cloud administration or live analytics.

## What it commits to

Ordinary list work remains available offline. Invalid import input leaves existing records unchanged. Users can inspect and export every stored record.

## What it refuses to be

It does not become an advertising service, mandatory cloud account or remote surveillance tool.

## What would prove this wrong

The direction fails if ordinary list work needs network access, groups cannot recover readable exports, or import validation silently loses existing records.

","a394c70c0de7a3aa6702c16dae5fec0733566bc6318aeff73e7faaf03bc6ebb0":"# Strategic positioning

`VISION.md` is the canonical project-vision artifact. This supporting rationale explains how the vision was derived and does not independently override it.

## Project category

Local-first record organizer.

## Primary user

Small volunteer groups maintaining lists on a shared computer.

## Primary pain

Hosted tools require accounts and reliable connectivity for simple record work.

## Primary promise

Useful, readable records remain under the group’s local control.

## Core mechanism

Import, validate and store locally, with inspectable export.

## Alternatives

Hosted spreadsheets offer richer simultaneous collaboration. Plain text offers less structured validation.

## Tradeoff

Local control and offline operation take priority over remote collaboration.

## Compatibility surfaces

Python, JSON files and command-line invocation are implementation compatibility choices, not the product category.

## Refusals

No mandatory cloud account, advertising or remote surveillance.

## Falsifiability

Ordinary use requiring network access, unrecoverable exports or silent record loss contradicts the promise.
